---
title: "Link-text collision diagnostics"
---

The pin for `TAL-LINK-TEXT`. This file must produce **exactly one** finding, and the
three cases below that do *not* fire are the load-bearing half: the rule fires nowhere in
the real 14-project corpus, so without a fixture that also carries its near-misses, a rule
that started over-firing would look identical to one that works. It lives in
`corpus/diagnostics/`, which is exempt from the corpus clean-render guards.

## The one finding: same words, different destination

Two links reading [the prose fixture](prose.tmd) and [the prose fixture](a11y.tmd) send a
reader to different documents under the same name, so neither one says where it goes.
That is the whole rule.

## Not a finding: two deep links into one document

A link to [the link fixture](links.tmd#broken-relative-links) and another to
[the link fixture](links.tmd#missing-local-video) name one destination twice. Destinations
are compared without their `#fragment`, so this is deliberately silent — the naive version
of this rule reports it, and on the dogfood books most of what it reported was exactly
this shape.

## Not a finding: the same words for the same place

Linking [the a11y fixture](a11y.tmd) here and [the a11y fixture](a11y.tmd) again is
repetition, not ambiguity: both go to one page, so the text is not lying to anyone.

## Not a finding: generated link text

Footnote references[^one] are generated links[^two] — the author does not write the "1"
or the "2", and cannot reword them. Two references to the *same* note read alike and
point at one anchor, which is repetition rather than ambiguity, so nothing is reported.
Generated links are exempt for that reason.

[^one]: The superscript marker in front of this note is a generated link.
[^two]: So is this one, pointing at a different anchor.
